infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title Five production failure modes — each a silent schema divergence with one fix
  desc Each is a place where the schema or query machinery quietly diverges from what the developer assumed, invisible until it hits production at scale
  lists
    - label Implicit type coercion kills the index
      desc WHERE user_id = '42' (string vs BIGINT) forces a per-row cast, so the planner cannot use the integer B-tree and falls to a sequential scan. Fix — typed query builders or ORMs that bind the correct parameter type.
    - label NULL semantics drop rows in joins
      desc A LEFT JOIN whose joined column is NULL gets silently dropped by a downstream inner JOIN, because NULL is not equal to anything, including itself. Fix — IS DISTINCT FROM for null-safe comparison and explicit COALESCE in WHERE.
    - label Unbounded cascade transactions
      desc DELETE on a heavily-cascaded root row builds a multi-million-row transaction that holds locks for minutes, blocking all writes to cascaded tables. Fix — soft-delete the root (deleted_at = now()), then batch-delete descendants 10,000 rows at a time.
    - label Schema drift via ad-hoc ALTER
      desc A direct ALTER TABLE in production outside the migration system desyncs the tool from reality, so future migrations fail mysteriously or produce inconsistent state. Fix — every schema change goes through migrations, enforced by CI.
    - label JSONB grown into relational shape
      desc A metadata JSONB column accreted 30 typed fields with schema-on-read and no enforcement, each feature adding metadata->>'feature_x'. Fix — the promote-to-column rule: the moment a field hits a WHERE clause, schedule a column-extraction migration.
theme
